<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-ph" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-chart.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-chart-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-chart.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/learning-center/">Learning center</a></li><li aria-current="page">When Appraisal Is the Wrong Tool for Your Insurance Claim</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 6 min read</p><h1>When Appraisal Is the Wrong Tool for Your Insurance Claim</h1><p class="lede">Appraisal works well for valuation disputes and poorly for almost everything else. Here’s how to recognize the situations where it won’t help.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Minnesota Insurance Appraiser team · Published <time datetime="2026-11-09">November 9, 2026</time> · 6 min read</p>
<p>An appraisal firm writing about when not to use appraisal may sound odd, but it is one of the most useful things we can explain. Appraisal is a strong tool for one specific problem: you and your insurer agree a loss is covered but disagree on how much it costs. Pointed at the wrong problem, it wastes time and money and can leave the real issue unresolved. Here are the situations where another route usually makes more sense.</p>

<h2>The claim has been denied</h2>
<p>If the insurer has denied the claim outright, the dispute is about coverage, not amount. An appraisal panel can set the value of a loss, but in Minnesota questions of what the policy covers stay with the courts. Even after <em>Quade v. Secura Insurance</em> (2012), which allows appraisers to address causation as part of the amount of loss, a panel’s award does not override a coverage denial.</p>
<p>Common denial scenarios include:</p>
<ul>
<li>The insurer says the loss is excluded, such as flood, surface water or earth movement</li>
<li>The insurer says the damage is wear and tear or maintenance</li>
<li>The insurer says the policy had lapsed or a condition was not met</li>
<li>The insurer alleges misrepresentation or fraud</li>
</ul>
<p>In these situations, speak with a Minnesota attorney. Water losses are a good example. Rising water like the 1997 Red River flood or the June 2012 Duluth flash flood raises exclusion questions that no appraiser can answer, and homeowners policies typically exclude flood unless separate flood coverage applies. Water that backed up through floor drains during those events raises yet another question: whether the policy carries a sewer backup endorsement and what its limit is. Those are coverage issues first.</p>
<h3>What to do after a denial</h3>
<p>Ask the insurer to put the denial and the policy language it relies on in writing. Gather your policy, photos, the adjuster’s report and any engineer’s findings. Then have an attorney review them. If coverage is later established, by agreement or by a court, appraisal may become useful for the valuation that remains.</p>

<h2>The gap is small</h2>
<p>Appraisal has real costs. Each side pays its own appraiser, and umpire fees are typically shared. If the difference between your number and the insurer’s is modest, those costs can consume most of what you might recover.</p>
<p>An illustrative example: if the gap is $3,000 and your share of appraisal costs is $2,500, the most you could gain is $500, and that assumes the panel agrees with everything you asked for. For small gaps, a well-documented supplement, a contractor meeting with the adjuster on site, or a complaint to the Minnesota Department of Commerce may be more efficient. Our <a href="/free-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap fee estimator</a> lets you test illustrative numbers.</p>

<h2>A deadline is close</h2>
<p>Standard policy language commonly limits suits to two years after the loss. Appraisal does not always pause that clock. If you are near a deadline and have unresolved coverage questions, starting appraisal could leave you without legal options once the period runs. Confirm deadlines in your policy and with an attorney before deciding.</p>
<h3>Other time-sensitive conditions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Proof of loss deadlines, if your policy requires one</li>
<li>Time limits for completing repairs to collect recoverable depreciation</li>
<li>Notice requirements for supplements or reopened claims</li>
</ul>

<h2>The dispute is about claim handling</h2>
<p>Sometimes the core complaint is how the claim was handled: long delays, unanswered calls, lost documents. Appraisal resolves value, not conduct. Concerns about claim handling can be raised with the Department of Commerce, and serious ones, such as an insurer denying benefits without a reasonable basis, may involve Minn. Stat. § 604.18. That statute carries procedural requirements and is a matter for an attorney.</p>

<h2>You haven’t tried a supplement yet</h2>
<p>Appraisal works best as a next step, not a first one. If you have not given the adjuster a written list of missing items with support, do that first. Many gaps close once the carrier sees a contractor’s itemized bid, code citations or photos of areas the first inspection missed. A <a href="/appraisal-solutions/claim-estimate-review/">claim estimate review</a> can help you build that request.</p>

<h2>The damage isn’t fully known</h2>
<p>An award fixes the amount of loss based on what the panel can see and document. If damage is still emerging, such as hidden water in walls after an ice dam or framing damage not yet opened up, it may be better to finish investigating before invoking appraisal. Otherwise you risk an award that misses items you later discover.</p>

<h2>Your goal is a repair, not a number</h2>
<p>Occasionally the real problem is a contractor who cannot start, a mortgage company holding the check or an insurer that needs a document from you. Those are logistics, not valuation disputes. A short call with the mortgage company’s loss draft department, or a written list of what the insurer still needs, often moves faster than any formal process.</p>

<h2>So when does appraisal fit?</h2>
<p>Appraisal is usually a good fit when the insurer has accepted the claim, you have already tried a supplement, the remaining gap is meaningful relative to the cost, the damage is well documented and no deadline is forcing a different path. If that describes your claim, the <a href="/appraisal-process-guide/">appraisal process guide</a> explains each step.</p>
<p>If you are unsure, Minnesota Insurance Appraiser is glad to look at your file and tell you honestly whether appraisal makes sense, even if the answer is no. <a href="/contact-us/">Contact us</a> or browse <a href="/common-questions/">common questions</a> and <a href="/insurance-terms/">insurance terms</a> first.</p>
